-- title:
--   Base-changed diamond automorphisms fix the Γ₀(N)-level functions
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and $L$ a field of characteristic zero. Write $F_0 =$ [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionField N`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L137) for the subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the ratios $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,\mathbb{Q}\,p_f / \mathrm{intSeriesC}\,\mathbb{Q}\,p_g$ attached to modular forms $f,g$ of some common weight $k$ for $\Gamma_1(N)$ having integral $q$-expansions $p_f,p_g$ with nonzero denominator series, and write `laurentBaseChange L` for the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the image of a given subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ under the coefficientwise map induced by $\mathbb{Q} \to L$. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L((q))/L$ with $K =$ `laurentBaseChange L` $F_0$, let $d$ be a natural number coprime to $N$, and let $\theta$ be an $L$-algebra automorphism of $K$ with the property that for all $x \in K$ and $x'$ in `laurentBaseChange L` $F_0$ having the same underlying Laurent series, $\theta(x)$ and `baseChangeAut L (diamondAut N d)` $x'$ have the same underlying Laurent series. Here `diamondAut N d` is a chosen $\mathbb{Q}$-automorphism $\sigma$ of $F_0$ satisfying `IsDiamondAut N d` — namely $d$ coprime to $N$ and, for all such $f,g,p_f,p_g$ and every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(N)$ with upper-left entry congruent to $d$ modulo $N$, the complex-coefficient image of $\sigma(p_f/p_g)$ times the $\gamma$-slashed $q$-expansion of $g$ in weight $k$ equals that of $f$ — and the identity if no such $\sigma$ exists; `baseChangeAut` similarly picks an $L$-automorphism of the base change acting on coefficient images by $\sigma$, and the identity if none exists. The conclusion is that every $u \in K$ whose Laurent series lies in `laurentBaseChange L` of `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ (CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma0 N)` — the $q$-expansion function field built in the same way from $\Gamma_0(N)$-forms — satisfies $\theta(u) = u$.
--
--   This is the statement that the diamond operator $\langle d \rangle$ on the function field of $X_1(N)$, and any base change of it to a characteristic-zero field $L$, acts trivially on functions of level $\Gamma_0(N)$ (in particular on $j$). It is used in the construction of Hecke and diamond correspondences on the two-chart models of $X_1$ over a base, where the diamond automorphisms must be seen to fix the $\Gamma_0(N)$-coordinates.
